#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/id #uuid "121d5f8c-3b38-5c75-aec1-646dc7d2f2f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14719" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063913 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "d113528f-a3ff-5f28-b071-bcf355a36b45" :question/math-academy-id "e-14720" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/id #uuid "9e531872-846f-5e75-97c0-dd2db3515558" :question/math-academy-id "e-14721" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "d38ac709-38e8-51a1-8e08-14913c77639c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14722" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b6f916d-f3e3-512b-a028-02b013492925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061295} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6fdd01-fed9-5174-8011-bdec233c5ef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061296} {:answer/id #uuid "e57cb073-71da-58d6-a9a7-bf9ce66c66e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061297} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2917cb-81d1-5307-acb1-1f7f0c085c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061298} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe95abb-6ad4-56f0-8716-fd0a07503731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fe95abb-6ad4-56f0-8716-fd0a07503731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061299} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d9afe78-8e13-5789-ac61-14e82575ffe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061294}] :question/id #uuid "0e3de030-cb78-5dd0-88f5-7229520c5041" :question/math-academy-id "q-15667" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/291/Source/Images/q-15667.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y = f(x)$. The point $A$ has coordinates $(3, 2)$ and lies on $y = f(x)$. Given that $(2, 3)$ lies on $y = f^{-1}(x)$, what is $(f^{-1})^{′}(2)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5110235-92c1-5a27-a99c-d71e2fe24850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061398} {:answer/id #uuid "ce568ea6-ae0b-5a67-87a9-94b73170df32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061399} {:answer/id #uuid "da1b6afb-a864-5662-b19c-7fa8166bccb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061400} {:answer/id #uuid "4e627842-4f81-542b-a973-27f010a478f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061401} {:answer/id #uuid "7c66de25-cfef-5e04-98e5-09487adc4a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e627842-4f81-542b-a973-27f010a478f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061401} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc3f4f87-07a3-561b-a1e5-5bea819e76c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34e33e76-2a83-5368-8561-def1a916040d" :question/math-academy-id "q-19201" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)=\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1,$ $g(2)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2) & =\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d59c5fa-d16d-5964-be9a-d24160ac2459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061721} {:answer/id #uuid "a27c6cb9-ef07-5ca8-9003-3fff68b1026f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061722} {:answer/id #uuid "f18bd396-41cf-5638-bf20-b68401a7d198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061723} {:answer/id #uuid "2f1d80e0-2855-553b-88c5-29018aa2e456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061724} {:answer/id #uuid "4299c6dd-fe94-5bce-a2c5-9538d740d7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4299c6dd-fe94-5bce-a2c5-9538d740d7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061725} :answer-field/id #uuid "6541b2f6-8aee-5eaf-9d8c-9e8524f4879b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061720}] :question/id #uuid "0e89d0fd-c938-54a1-9b82-cef992b23366" :question/math-academy-id "q-2294" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{3} + 3$ and the point $P(2, 11)$ that lies on $y = f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P$, calculate $(f^{-1})^{′}(11)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe6726f-14a9-5251-a055-84ac34260be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295810} {:answer/id #uuid "0f65b70c-4513-5d7f-88de-bf7e421f1942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe6726f-14a9-5251-a055-84ac34260be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295810} :answer-field/id #uuid "40350093-241a-55dd-ac9f-9a83f78e707e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c43205-3a68-5865-9baf-3488c8c39bcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-312578" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5595ee00-3e08-5c85-ab82-a1dbb68af433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322613} {:answer/id #uuid "83ac79de-d929-59e9-b4cd-f03e4d332524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5595ee00-3e08-5c85-ab82-a1dbb68af433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322613} :answer-field/id #uuid "822f713b-c25e-5da8-a950-9db41fcefb5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1f6eff9-e354-50e5-b0d6-7aed9742e28f" :question/math-academy-id "q-312579" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and the point $P(2,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0464f1b-b508-5d46-aab4-a160247b57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0464f1b-b508-5d46-aab4-a160247b57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295818} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd9d412-937f-5c3d-b8be-8ecf613b4921" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1490c31-6761-5f3f-8b46-c37dbd062af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-312583" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77556d4a-1589-5b09-bec1-8ce736a069a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77556d4a-1589-5b09-bec1-8ce736a069a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295824} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e16358-519e-5455-b910-148e3f8eb26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "422cbd5e-38e5-544a-b056-53061bf5421a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342562" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205c7a5e-3ebd-5d2e-8080-e01818835b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "205c7a5e-3ebd-5d2e-8080-e01818835b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295834} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aee9e45-6561-505c-b53a-1631b71fe964" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "693dd3d0-3454-5894-a36e-0df2728d3681" :question/math-academy-id "q-342583" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93df8dc9-5514-5610-9ba7-f8141c1ad15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063694} {:answer/id #uuid "aa054826-9bc5-55fa-91f8-c8a1331439de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063695} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9e50e9-5afc-5ec8-9608-e1478bb62e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063696} {:answer/id #uuid "02eb72f8-e800-5b85-8c72-f9a7b308c956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063697} {:answer/id #uuid "19c1d878-07a5-503d-8555-81b1fc1b9466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c1d878-07a5-503d-8555-81b1fc1b9466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063698} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a4358ed-8a57-5c21-93f2-ea7a5edec4a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dd725b3-7f44-5fca-9ff0-b43869ecbad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75986" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=5.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)+\\frac{x}{x-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4)+\\frac{5}{5-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59821fc8-e9df-5397-8f51-eb936149f62d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295855} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d66dab-93a0-5e57-b08d-c5fef1224017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295856} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e7b9cc-271b-5641-9a48-5cb756f4d2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295857} {:answer/id #uuid "3bea40ac-6a9a-5644-8b15-d06af405881c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295858} {:answer/id #uuid "35a03d42-f1c5-5c7f-ba9d-b0db949ac390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e7b9cc-271b-5641-9a48-5cb756f4d2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295857} :answer-field/id #uuid "521de4c2-d1f7-5a30-9d43-b3d0a36305f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8be70d73-596d-5442-ba07-c1e2659c3b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76128" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=1+2{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ 1+2{e}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(3)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =1+2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
